          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of structures that are an archetypal model of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} mathematical structure
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+construction"} free
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            form of such a kind of structure.
          %h2#terminology Terminology
          %p There are several terms used in the category theory literature for this concept:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong free-living
                (e.g.
                = succeed "))" do
                  %a{:href => "#FLK80"} Foltz, Lair, &amp; Kelly 1980
            %li
              %p
                %strong free-standing
            %li
              %p
                %strong walking
                (e.g.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#HLOR24"} Hadzihasanovic et al 2024
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#OR24"} Ozornova &amp; Rovelli 2024
          %p
            The term
            %em walking
            is
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/01/f_and_the_shibboleth.html#c031066"} believed
            to have been introduced by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Dolan"} James Dolan
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/revision/walking+structure/1"} in 2010
          %p
            The idea of “walking” here is as in “John is a walking almanac” or “
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eugene_Levy"} Eugene Levy
            is a walking pair of eyebrows” (which may be the original motivation given by Dolan).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p The idea is probably easier to apprehend through examples rather than through a formal definition, but for the record:
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a type of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} mathematical structure
            that can be defined in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category"} higher category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            %em walking X
            refers to the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+construction"} free
              category (resp. higher category, category with suitable structure) containing an
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            More precisely, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi StructCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} StructCat
            denotes some (higher) category of categories with an appropriate type of structure, then the
            %strong walking X
            is an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi StructCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X] \in StructCat
            together with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi StructCat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %mi Xs
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi in
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  StructCat([X],C)
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  \\big\{Xs \; in \; C\}
          %p
            between the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-space"} space
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X]
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi StructCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\in StructCat
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              In other words, the structured category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X]
              equipped with its canonical type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
              among such structured categories that come equipped with such types
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X]
              ‘coclassifies’ such types: this is analogous to how a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B G
              for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              classifies
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundles"} bundles
              = succeed "-bundle" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: E \to X
              over a suitable space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              has a classifying map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_p: X \to B G
              (unique up to homotopy) such that pulling back the canonical
              = succeed "-bundle" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi: E G \to B G
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_p
              reproduces the type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "http://nforum.mathforge.org/discussion/1529/walking-structures/#Item_5"} comment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X]
              along a structured-category morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X] \to C
              to obtain a given type of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+morphism"} walking morphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                in a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+isomorphism"} walking isomorphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
                in a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} Equivalences
                in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                are represented by the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+equivalence"} walking equivalence
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adj"} Adj
                is the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+adjunction"} walking adjunction
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+2-isomorphism+with+trivial+boundary"} walking 2-isomorphism with trivial boundary
                is a 2-groupoid model for the 2-truncation of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sphere"} 2-sphere
            %li
              %p
                The augmented/algebraist’s
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
                is “the
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %em
                    walking
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the"} the
                monoidal category that results if all one need be told about it is that it has a monoid object – all the morphisms of the category are obtainable from the monoid structure by applying the operations of a monoidal category, and they are subject to no further relations beyond those implied by the monoid axioms.
            %li
              %p
                Similarly, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
                of groups can be described as “the
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %em
                    walking
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+categories"} cartesian monoidal categories
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
          %p
            The last two examples indicate the need for a little care: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
            or type of structured category in which the ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            ’ of “the walking
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+categories"} finitely complete categories
          %p
            If the doctrine is not specified, then a reasonable default is a ‘minimal’ doctrine in which the concept makes sense; for example, to make sense of monoids, one doesn’t need more than monoidal categories. See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcosm+principle"} microcosm principle
          %p Thus, more generally,
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              in some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is the “walking
              = succeed "-model\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              = succeed "-category)." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is “the
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %em
                  walking
                  = succeed "-model" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %em qua
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
              (where the morphisms are the left-adjoint parts of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
          %h2#relation_to_initial_objects Relation to initial objects
          %p
            The walking X is, of course, not the same as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            X.
          %p
            Consider for example the case when X is a pointed monoid (a monoid equipped with an element). The initial pointed monoid (in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            equipped with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\in \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_M
            with finite products containing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\in C_M
            and an “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"}> element
            ”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e:1\to M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            has a universal property mapping into other pointed monoids in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_M
            has a universal property mapping into other categories with products equipped with pointed monoids.
          %p
            Nevertheless, the first sits inside the second! Specifically, for any category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with products, the “underlying set” functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(1,-):C\to Set
            is product-preserving and hence carries monoid objects to monoid objects, and in the case of the walking pointed monoid we have
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_M(1,M) \cong \mathbb{N}
          %p
            This is true rather generally:
            = succeed "." do
              %em the initial X is the underlying X of the walking X
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              containing an object
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %ol
              %li
                The domain projection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo ⫽
                      %mi S
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\sslash S \to K
                from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+slice+2-category"} lax slice 2-category
                has a section. Explicitly, for every object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                we have a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_X : X\to S
                and for every morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
                we have a 2-cell
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi σ
                          %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mi Y
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_f : s_X \to s_Y f
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo :
                      %mi f
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha :f\to g
                we have
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi σ
                          %mi g
                        %mo .
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mi Y
                        %mi α
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi σ
                          %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_g . s_Y\alpha = \sigma_f
              %li
                We have
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mi S
                        %mo ≅
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_S \cong 1_S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                the composite
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi s
                                %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi S
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi X
                      %mo ≅
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_X \xrightarrow{\sigma_{s_X}} s_S s_X \cong s_X
                is the identity.
            %p
              Then for any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_X:X\to S
              is the initial object of the hom-category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X,S)
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              We will use the characterization of initial objects via
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object#ConesOverTheIdentity"} cones over the identity
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_{s_X} : K(X,S) \to K(X,S)
              to the identity, which is the identity at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to S
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mi f
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi S
                      %mi f
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_X \xrightarrow{\sigma_f} s_S f \cong f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi g
                    %mo .
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi Y
                    %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_g . s_Y\alpha = \sigma_f
              makes this a natural transformation; and the final assumption says exactly that this is the identity at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_X
          %p
            To see how this theorem implies that the initial X is the underlying X of the walking X, consider again the case of pointed monoids. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            be the 2-category of categories with products and product-preserving functors, let
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S=Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi s
                    %mi C
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_C : C \to Set
            be
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(1,-)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(1,-)
            is the initial functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to Set
            for any
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Now take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to be the walking pointed monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_M
            above. Then its universal property tells us that functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C_M\to Set
            are equivalent to pointed monoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(M)
            in
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(1,M)
            is the initial pointed monoid in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %p
            A similar argument applies whenever we have a “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            \-like” object of a 2-category with “underlying set” morphisms to it. For instance, in place of categories with finite products we could consider categories with finite limits. However, in other cases, such as monoidal categories, there is a disconnect: the natural “underlying set” functor for a monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(I,-)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is the unit object; but in general this is only
            %em lax
            monoidal, whereas the universal property of a “walking X qua monoidal category” is relative to
            %em strong
            monoidal functors.
          %p
            It does often happen that this disconnect can be bridged. For instance, suppose X is something that can be defined in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
            (like a pointed monoid). Then we can apply the above argument to the 2-category of multicategories, as the walking X qua multicategory has a universal property relative to functors of multicategories, even though such functors correspond to
            %em lax
            functors between monoidal categories. It follows that the underlying set functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ;
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_M(;-) : C_M\to Set
            of the walking X qua multicategory corresponds to the initial X in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            Moreover, we can deduce from this that the underlying pointed monoid of the walking X qua monoidal category is also the initial X in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            that generates walking Xs, the latter fact can be seen as a sort of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonicity"} canonicity
            for the tensor-product constructor.
          %p
            Similar arguments apply in other cases. For instance, in the 2-category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
            we can take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to be the double category
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Span"} Span
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+categories"} virtual double categories
            instead.
          %p
            On the other hand, there are also cases where this argument does not apply. For instance, X could be something that can be defined in a monoidal category but
            %em not
            in a multicategory, such as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monoid"} Frobenius monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(I,-)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(I,-)
            preserves Xs, there is a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategory"} generalized multicategory
            in which Xs can be defined and this argument carried through, but I do not know of a general theory suggesting this.
